1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:08,566
The brush reveals a smooth, polished axe, ground on a stone until its edge was sharp. This is a Neolithic tool.

2
00:00:08,566 --> 00:00:14,654
People who made it were no longer only hunting. They were planting seeds, and keeping animals.

3
00:00:14,654 --> 00:00:17,758
What changed when people began to farm?

4
00:00:31,750 --> 00:00:40,656
How do we know? Again, from the ground. Archaeologists dug up pits, post-holes, burnt grains and animal bones.

5
00:00:40,656 --> 00:00:46,988
At some sites even the burials show how people lived. There are still no written records.

6
00:00:59,483 --> 00:01:07,814
After the Palaeolithic comes the Mesolithic, from about ten thousand years ago. Tools became tiny stone blades called microliths.

7
00:01:07,814 --> 00:01:15,956
People still hunted and gathered, but they also herded animals. At Bhimbetka, rock paintings show hunters and dances.

8
00:01:15,956 --> 00:01:22,802
Then came the Neolithic, the New Stone Age. The best-known early site is Mehrgarh, in Baluchistan.

9
00:01:22,802 --> 00:01:31,006
Its excavators dated it to about seven thousand BCE, though newer radiocarbon work suggests about five thousand BCE.

10
00:01:31,006 --> 00:01:35,424
Here people grew wheat and barley, and lived in mud-brick houses.

11
00:01:35,424 --> 00:01:42,918
At Burzahom in Kashmir, people lived in pits dug into the ground. Dogs were buried with their masters.

12
00:01:42,918 --> 00:01:47,732
At Koldihwa in Uttar Pradesh, grains of rice have been found.

13
00:01:47,732 --> 00:01:54,143
In the south, at Maski, Brahmagiri and Piklihal in Karnataka, people kept cattle.

14
00:01:54,143 --> 00:02:01,937
They left behind ash mounds, which are heaps of burnt dung. Neolithic people ground and polished their stone axes.

15
00:02:01,937 --> 00:02:10,540
They also made pottery, and ground grain on stone querns. Farming meant a fixed home, stored food, and in time, villages.

16
00:02:10,540 --> 00:02:14,096
This is the foundation of everything that follows.

17
00:02:26,617 --> 00:02:36,105
Old name, new name. Many books say New Stone Age. The scholarly word is Neolithic, from Greek words meaning new stone.

18
00:02:36,105 --> 00:02:41,141
It is the age of polished tools, pottery and the first farms.

19
00:02:53,650 --> 00:03:02,841
Carve this tip into memory. Mesolithic: tiny tools. Neolithic: polished axes, pots, farms.

20
00:03:02,841 --> 00:03:09,585
Tiny blades first, then polished axes and farms. Put them in that order in your answer.

21
00:03:22,083 --> 00:03:38,617
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. Name this smooth, ground tool of the Neolithic age. The answer: a polished stone axe.

22
00:03:38,617 --> 00:03:52,629
Number two. This tiny blade was set into a handle. What is such a tool called? The answer: a microlith. Number three.

23
00:03:52,629 --> 00:04:05,782
Which site in Baluchistan shows the earliest farming in South Asia? The answer: Mehrgarh. Now one line for Mains.

24
00:04:05,782 --> 00:04:09,571
Discuss the importance of the Neolithic revolution.

25
00:04:26,083 --> 00:04:32,766
The polished axe goes into its case, next to the hand-axe, for comparison. Three facts to take away.

26
00:04:32,766 --> 00:04:41,476
Ground and polished, not chipped. Neolithic farmers settled at Mehrgarh. Wheat, barley, pottery and villages appear.

27
00:04:41,476 --> 00:04:45,345
Next gallery: Copper and Painted Pots.

